"finding": "unconditional_excluded",
"comment": "The guideline states a person is not fit for an unconditional licence if they have any visual field defect. The nurse note confirms the driver has 'a blurred patch off to one side' and lacks a formal binocular field result, but does not confirm the presence of a specific named defect (e.g., scotoma). However, the absence of a confirmed normal field or the description of a patchy loss suggests a potential defect exists. The note explicitly states there is 'no formal binocular field result', meaning the criterion for meeting the conditional standard (binocular field extent ≥140°) cannot be met."
},
{
"source_slot": 2,
"patient_paragraph": 2,
"applicability": "unresolved",
"finding": "unconditional_excluded",
"comment": "The guideline defines normal visual field as extending from 160 to 200 degrees horizontally. The nurse note states the driver has 'no formal binocular field result'. Without this data, it is impossible to verify if the extent meets the ≥140° requirement within 10° of the midline."
},
{
"source_slot": 3,
"patient_paragraph": 2,
"applicability": "unresolved",
"finding": "unconditional_excluded",
"comment": "The guideline requires visual acuity of 6/9 or better in the better eye (with or without correction) for a conditional licence. The nurse note lists 'right 6/12 and left 6/18' but does not identify if these are corrected or uncorrected measurements. If uncorrected, the right eye fails the 6/9 standard. Even if corrected, the lack of clarification prevents establishing compliance."
},
{
"source_slot": 4,
"patient_paragraph": 2,
"applicability": "unresolved",
"finding": "unconditional_excluded",
"comment": "The guideline requires assessment of 'any central loss that is an extension of a hemianopia or quadrantanopia'. The nurse note mentions a 'blurred patch off to one side' but does not characterize it as a central loss, nor does it provide the specialist assessment required to confirm the nature and size of any field defect."
},
{
"source_slot": 5,
"patient_paragraph": 1,
"applicability": "unresolved",
"finding": "unconditional_excluded",
"comment": "The guideline states people with diplopia within 20 degrees of central fixation are generally not fit to drive and should be referred. The nurse note reports the driver explains signs appear as two overlapping images after several hours, improving when an eye is covered, but 'has not established the angle from central fixation'. Therefore, it cannot be confirmed if the diplopia falls within the critical 20-degree zone."
},
{
"source_slot": 6,
"patient_paragraph": 1,
"applicability": "unresolved",
"finding": "unconditional_excluded",
"comment": "The guideline requires a three-month non-driving period for diplopia managed with an occluder to re-establish depth perception. The nurse note states the symptom 'improving when either eye is covered' but does not confirm if the driver has undergone this specific management protocol or the required waiting period."
}
],
"fitness": "undetermined",
"outcome": "insufficient_information",
"explanation": "The assessment cannot determine fitness because critical data is missing from the nurse note. Specifically, there is no formal binocular visual field result to confirm the ≥140° extent required for a conditional licence, nor is it clear if the reported 'blurred patch' constitutes a disqualifying defect or meets the static/progression criteria. Additionally, the status of the visual acuity measurements (corrected vs uncorrected) is unknown, and the specific angle of central fixation for the reported diplopia has not been established to determine if it falls within the 20-degree exclusion zone. The absence of these specific test results prevents confirming either unconditional clearance or conditional eligibility.","missing_information": ["Formal binocular visual field result (extent ≥140°)", "Confirmation of whether 'blurred patch' is a named defect (scotoma, etc.)", "Visual acuity status (corrected vs uncorrected) for both eyes", "Angle of central fixation for diplopia symptoms", "Evidence of 3-month non-driving period if occluder therapy was used"]}
